WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.560
I do tend to subscribe to the idea of not having or focusing on regrets because everything we've done

00:00:07.929 --> 00:00:12.539
sort of makes us who we are now, and I'm happy with who I am now and

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:17.368
especially in terms of my academic pathway and my career in physics.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.209
But if I was to look back at my undergrad degree in

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:26.279
hindsight, there are probably things I would try to do differently.

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:31.229
I don't know how they would affect where I do end up. Maybe it would be better,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.369
maybe it would be worse, but there are probably things I would try to change.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.750
So one of them is probably trying to do

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:44.039
more courses in or to do a minor in computer science.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:50.669
So I've spoken about the importance of coding and computer science in terms of physics before

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:58.230
but I do think it is really important and becoming more so. I took some courses in computer science

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:04.680
but not enough for a minor or a major, I instead did my majors in physics and math.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:09.688
It's hard to say with this one because to have done a

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.739
major or a minor in the computer science as well,

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:21.749
like I was already taking more courses than you're supposed to every semester, so I would have had to cut something out.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.750
Maybe cut the maths out

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:30.060
and I probably could have done without the maths since I'm doing an experimental physics project for my PhD,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:38.579
but I enjoyed the maths all the same. In some ideal situation I would have had enough room to do

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:47.069
the triple major without adding any more time on to my degree, like another year or another semester because I didn't want to do that.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:48.720
But yeah,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.459
I think ideally I would have done some more of those comp sci courses.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.110
The second thing I wanted to talk about was kind of my approach

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:01.289
to studying at university and

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:05.219
it came down to essentially being a

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.439
memorization and cramming task for every exam period.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:17.040
At the time this felt like the best way to get through because we had so many

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:21.180
assignments and so many courses that I always found myself

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.140
sort of disregarding some of the really deep

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:33.270
connections and and meanings behind what we were doing and just focused on, how do I get the best grade in the exam?

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.460
And that really became my focus especially in exam period and I don't

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:46.020
like that, especially in hindsight. I look back, and I think I really wish I actually understood that concept more even though

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.770
I got a good grade on it.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:52.529
I don't know, like with this it seems to be almost a systemic

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.549
thing sort of across people and across courses

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.960
that

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:05.549
the uni system is focused more on outcomes and grades than it is on your deeper understanding.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:10.770
So I don't know if this was entirely my fault or just the way

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.950
kind of things have to be if you want to get through.

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:22.589
It's also hard to look back and say I would change that approach because I did get good grades throughout undergrad

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.110
and if I'd taken time to

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.779
really slow down and really learn, I

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.360
don't know if I would have had time to

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.970
do everything that I needed to do, so it's a bit of both, but definitely

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:42.809
I look back, and I think I probably wish that I asked more questions. More questions of my friends

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.810
but also of the lecturers

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.080
and

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:53.249
to really try to understand deeply the content rather than being happy with

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.699
just getting a good grade on it. I think the most common questions that I ask my friends are just,

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.660
Hey, how do you do question 3 on the assignment?

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.940
I think now that I'm a PhD student

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:14.119
I have more time to investigate those deeper learnings, and so that's something I'm trying to do now

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.830
because in hindsight I didn't really get the time to

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:25.310
appreciate it as an undergrad. I hope that that helps someone out there and thanks for watching

